  "body": "Part of #2574 (research answer 2).\n\nThe maintainer named the Solidcraft route in #2574: \"Or use solidcrafts model with the joints defined in the solidcraft file. Either way i guess. if we go the solidcraft route a base model could be generated thru its MCP.\"\n\n## Recommended in #2574\nThe contract is a file, not a live MCP call. Solidcraft (MIT OR Apache-2.0) exports an assembly with its joints as URDF (`export` tool, `docs/mcp.md` of tangency-labs/solidcraft). URDF is a public XML format (wiki.ros.org/urdf/XML): links, `revolute` and `prismatic` joints, `origin`, `axis`, `limit`, mesh references. `camcraft-sim` reads only the file and never needs Solidcraft at run time. Without a URDF, the model comes from the `.mch` (#2584, #2591).\n\n## Open design points\n1. The `.mch` key that names the URDF and maps each URDF joint to an axis letter.\n2. The units and the root frame of the URDF against the machine frame and the `travel` of the `.mch`. Which file wins when the joint limits differ.\n3. The mesh formats to read (STL and OBJ readers exist; GLB does not).\n4. Refusals: a joint with no letter, a letter with no joint, a closed chain, a mesh that is not found.\n\n## Done means\nThe design points have answers in comments, and the code sub-issue is filed with a `P*`, `effort:*` and `area:*` label. No hard dependency on Solidcraft is added.\n",
